+    segments: [
+      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
+        "An evaluation — an eval — is a fixed set of test cases plus a defined way of grading them. It is the difference between 'this prompt feels better' and 'this prompt scores 84% against the same 60 cases the old one scored 71% on'.",
+        "The reason evals exist is regression: a change that fixes the case in front of you routinely breaks three cases you are not looking at. Without a fixed set you never find out. Iterating on impressions feels fast and quietly is not." ] },
+      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
+        "Start with success criteria, not with metrics. Good criteria are specific ('the summary names every policy clause referenced in the source'), measurable, achievable given current models, and relevant to what the application is actually for. 'Accurate' and 'high quality' are not criteria; they are placeholders for criteria you have not written yet.",
+        "There are three ways to grade, and the order matters. Code-based grading — exact match, string presence, a schema check — is fastest, cheapest and most reliable, so use it wherever the task allows. Human grading is the highest quality and the least scalable; spend it where judgement is genuinely required. LLM-based grading, often called LLM-as-a-judge, sits between them: fast and flexible, but it is a model with the same failure modes as the model under test, so give it an explicit rubric, ask it to reason before scoring, and validate the judge itself against a set of human-labelled cases before trusting it at scale.",
+        "For a pipeline, evaluate the components separately or you will misdiagnose everything. A RAG answer can be wrong because the right passage was never retrieved, or because the passage was retrieved and the model ignored it. Those are different bugs with different fixes, and one overall score hides which one you have. Frameworks such as Ragas exist precisely to split that measurement: context precision and context recall describe retrieval, faithfulness and answer relevancy describe generation.",
+        "Two practical notes. Prefer volume over polish: fifty automatically graded cases beat eight hand-graded ones for catching regressions. And build the eval set from real failures — the questions your users actually asked and got a bad answer to — rather than from cases you invented, which tend to be the cases you already handle." ] },
