+      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
+        "Classical application security rests on keeping code and data apart: a database knows a query is a query and a value is a value. A language model has no such separation. Your instructions and the content it reads arrive as one stream of tokens, and the model has no reliable way to tell which is which.",
+        "That single fact generates most of the security problems in this field. OWASP maintains a Top 10 for LLM applications, and prompt injection has been number one in every edition." ] },
+      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
+        "Direct injection is a user typing something that overrides your instructions. Indirect injection is the serious one: hostile text hidden in a document, a web page, an email or a calendar invite that your system reads. The attacker never talks to your application; they only need to put words somewhere it will look. Day 20's rule — treat everything the system reads as data, never as instructions — is the mitigation, and it is a mitigation rather than a cure. There is no known prompt wording that reliably makes a model ignore instructions embedded in its context.",
+        "So the defences live outside the prompt. Mark boundaries explicitly with delimiters so content is visibly content. Give each tool the least privilege that still works, since an injected instruction can only do what your tools permit. Require human approval before anything consequential or irreversible. Cap iterations and spend. Log every tool call so you can reconstruct what happened.",
+        "Improper output handling is the mirror image and is under-rated. Model output passed into SQL, HTML, a shell command or a downstream API is untrusted input from a security point of view, no matter how friendly it looks. Every classical injection vulnerability is available again, with the model as the source.",
+        "Then there is data. Anything you paste into a hosted assistant leaves your machine; the provider's retention and training settings decide what happens next, and those are a decision to make deliberately rather than a default to inherit. Learner records are personal data, so minimise what you send, avoid names where an identifier would do, and remember that prompts and logs are storage too. Secrets follow Day 5's rule: keys live on the server, never in frontend code, never committed to Git, and rotated when in doubt.",
+        "Finally, frameworks. NIST's AI Risk Management Framework organises this work into govern, map, measure and manage — useful less as a checklist than as a shared vocabulary when you have to argue for controls in an organisation that has not thought about this yet." ] },
+      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
+        "Audit yourself first. List what you have pasted into an AI assistant in the last month, and mark anything that was someone else's personal data or an employer's confidential material.",
