+        "What it changes is the cost of production. That is a real shift, and it carries a specific risk: when producing content becomes cheap, the constraint that used to force prioritisation disappears, and organisations produce more material without producing more learning." ] },
+      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
+        "Map the technology onto the workflow rather than onto the job title. In analysis, a model is good at clustering thirty interview transcripts into themes and bad at telling you what learners need — one is compression of evidence you have, the other is invention. In design, it drafts objectives and generates scenario variations quickly, and you keep the decision about what matters. In development it produces first-draft scripts, distractors, alt text and translation drafts. In evaluation it clusters free-text feedback into patterns a human then reads.",
+        "The safe pattern is the one from Day 15, stated as a rule: a human-owned source of truth, model-assisted drafting, human review before publication, and traceability for anything that will be assessed. Reverse any of those and the failure is not dramatic — it is a course that is subtly wrong in ways nobody notices until a learner acts on it.",
+        "Grounding beats instructing. Asking a model to write scenario items 'about our refund policy' invites plausible invention; supplying the policy text and requiring each item to name the clause it tests moves the work from recall to reading, which is the task models are far more reliable at. It also makes review fast, because a reviewer can check a citation in seconds and a claim in minutes.",
+        "Then assessment integrity. If an assessment can be completed by pasting its prompt into a chatbot, it was probably measuring the retrieval of retrievable facts. Detection tools are unreliable and put you in an adversarial relationship with learners; redesign is the durable answer. Shift weight toward process artefacts, critique of a supplied flawed example, application to the learner's own context, defence of a decision, or work produced in a supervised session. Note the analogy to this app: its own short answers are keyword-graded and therefore gameable, which is exactly why they carry less weight than the scenario items.",
+        "Finally, policy is part of the design. UNESCO's guidance for generative AI in education argues for human agency, data-privacy protection, age-appropriate use, and validating a tool's pedagogical fitness before adoption. Whether or not you agree with every recommendation, citing a recognised framework is how a proposal stops being a personal opinion inside an institution." ] },
+      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
+        "Take one real objective from a course you have built. Ask an assistant for five multiple-choice distractors for it, giving it the source material.",
+        "Score each distractor against three rules: plausible to someone who has not learned it, unambiguously wrong to someone who has, and not eliminable by length or grammar alone. Count survivors. That survival rate is your calibration for how much review this shortcut actually needs." ] },
+      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
+        "Take one assessment you own that a learner could now complete with a chatbot in two minutes.",
+        "Rewrite the task so that the same objective is measured but the model's fluency is no longer sufficient. Change the task, not the rules about tools." ] },
+      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
+        "Of what the model produced today, what did you keep and what did you discard? The pattern in what you discard is a description of your own expertise — write one sentence naming it." ] },
+    ],
+    example: {
+      t: "Grounding a scenario writer",
+      body: "A team drafting a compliance refresher asks a model for forty scenario questions on their refund policy. The output is fluent and the subject-matter expert rejects a substantial share of it: the scenarios describe a policy that sounds like most refund policies rather than theirs. The second attempt supplies the policy document and requires each item to quote the clause it tests. Rejection drops sharply, and review speeds up more than drafting did, because a reviewer now checks a citation rather than reconstructing the reasoning. The lesson generalises past e-learning: when a model invents, the cheapest fix is usually to give it the source rather than to describe the source better.",
+    },
+    practice: {
+      t: "A review gate someone else can run",
+      steps: [
+        "Write a three-step check that a colleague could apply to AI-drafted assessment items without asking you anything.",
+        "Make at least one step a rule a non-expert can apply, and one step something only a subject-matter expert can judge.",
