+  {
+    day: 24,
+    title: "AI, the LMS and Learning Data",
+    sub: "lms",
+    objectives: [
+      "Describe what data an LMS already produces, and what it structurally cannot tell you.",
+      "Distinguish SCORM from xAPI by the question each one can answer.",
+      "Decide where AI plugs into a learning stack without becoming the system of record.",
+    ],
+    segments: [
+      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
+        "An LMS is a system of record: enrolments, completions, scores, dates. That is a narrow but authoritative slice of reality, and its authority is the reason it exists.",
+        "Most ambitious 'AI in the LMS' ideas fail before the model is even chosen, because the data needed to make them work was never recorded. A completion flag and a final score cannot tell you what a learner did, where they hesitated, or which objective they failed twice." ] },
+      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
+        "SCORM, the older standard, defines a small fixed vocabulary a course package reports back to the LMS: completed, passed, a score, time spent. It is well supported and it answers exactly one class of question — did this person finish this thing.",
+        "xAPI takes a different shape. A statement is roughly actor, verb, object — 'Rina attempted question 4' — and statements are sent to a Learning Record Store, an LRS, which is a separate system from the LMS. Because the vocabulary is open and the source does not have to be a course, xAPI can describe simulations, VR sessions, coaching conversations and on-the-job actions. That openness is also its cost: with no agreed vocabulary inside your organisation, two teams will record the same event differently and the data will not aggregate. Choosing xAPI is choosing to do design work that SCORM did for you.",
+        "Where AI belongs in this stack. Content generation sits outside, producing material that is reviewed and then imported. An assistant over course materials is the RAG pattern from Day 19, reading your content rather than the model's memory. Open-response scoring can be assisted, with the human as the decision-maker and an eval from Day 21 measuring whether the assistance is any good. Analytics over LRS data can cluster behaviour and surface patterns worth a human's attention.",
+        "Where it does not belong: as the system of record for a grade, and as an unreviewed decision-maker about a person. An automated 'at risk' label attached to a learner is a consequential decision, and the controls are the same as any consequential automation — evidence, appeal, human sign-off.",
+        "Privacy sits underneath all of it. Learner records are personal data, and sending them to a hosted model means a third party now processes them. Minimise fields, prefer identifiers to names, decide retention deliberately, and remember from Day 22 that prompts and logs are storage too." ] },
+      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
+        "Write three xAPI-shaped statements for an activity in a course you know, in actor-verb-object form.",
+        "Beside each, write the question it would let you answer that a completion flag cannot. If you cannot write that question, the statement is not worth recording — and that filter is the whole skill." ] },
+      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
+        "Pick one AI feature you would genuinely want in your LMS.",
+        "Write three lines: what data it needs, whether that data exists today, and what you would have to instrument to get it. Most ideas die on line two, and finding that out on paper is far cheaper than finding it out in a pilot." ] },
+      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
+        "If you could add exactly one new tracked event to a course you run, which one, and what decision would it let you make differently?" ] },
+    ],
+    example: {
+      t: "The at-risk flag that flagged everybody",
+      body: "A training team wants to identify learners at risk of dropping out. The only data available is SCORM completion percentage, so the first version flags everyone in week one — completion is low for all learners early, and the signal is dominated by the calendar rather than by behaviour. The signals that would actually work — repeated failed attempts on the same objective, long gaps between sessions, starting and abandoning the same module — were never recorded, because nothing in the content sent them. The project's real problem was instrumentation, and no model choice could have rescued it. Note that this app makes the opposite choice deliberately: it stores raw events and derives progress, so a new metric can be computed later without re-collecting anything.",
+    },
+    practice: {
+      t: "Instrument before you model",
+      steps: [
+        "Choose one question you would like to answer about your learners, phrased so a yes or no answer would change something you do.",
+        "List the events you would need recorded to answer it, and mark each as available today, obtainable with work, or not obtainable.",
+        "Write one sentence on what you would do differently if the answer turned out to be the opposite of what you expect.",
+      ],
+    },
+    challenge: "Design the minimum instrumentation for a study assistant that can honestly tell a learner 'you are weak on embeddings'. Specify what must be recorded, how many data points before the claim is fair, and how the learner clears the flag. Then open this app's review system and compare: it makes all three of those decisions, and you can now judge whether it made them well.",
+    recap: {
+      inti: "LMS adalah sistem pencatat resmi: pendaftaran, penyelesaian, skor, tanggal. Otoritatif tetapi sempit. SCORM melaporkan kosakata kecil yang sudah ditetapkan — selesai, lulus, skor, durasi — dan hanya bisa menjawab satu jenis pertanyaan. xAPI merekam pernyataan berbentuk pelaku-kata kerja-objek ke sebuah Learning Record Store, sehingga bisa menggambarkan pengalaman di luar kursus, dengan konsekuensi lo sendiri yang harus menyepakati kosakatanya. AI menempel di sekitar tumpukan ini: pembuatan konten, asisten berbasis RAG atas materi, bantuan penilaian, dan analitik atas data LRS.",
+      salahPaham: "Kebanyakan ide \"AI di LMS\" gagal bukan karena modelnya kurang pintar, tetapi karena datanya tidak pernah direkam. Bendera penyelesaian dan skor akhir tidak bisa memberi tahu apa yang dilakukan pembelajar. Instrumentasi lebih dulu, model belakangan — dan jangan pernah menjadikan AI sebagai pemegang catatan nilai yang resmi.",
+      istilah: [
+        { en: "system of record", id: "sistem yang memegang catatan resmi dan menjadi rujukan akhir" },
+        { en: "SCORM", id: "standar lama; kosakata pelaporan tetap dan terbatas ke LMS" },
+        { en: "xAPI", id: "standar perekaman pengalaman belajar berbentuk pelaku-kata kerja-objek" },
+        { en: "Learning Record Store", id: "penyimpan pernyataan xAPI; terpisah dari LMS" },
+        { en: "instrument", id: "memasang perekaman peristiwa yang belum tercatat" },
+      ],
+    },
+    resources: [
+      { title: "xAPI (Experience API) explained", source: "xAPI.com (Rustici Software)", topic: "AI + LMS", difficulty: "Beginner", minutes: 35, url: "https://xapi.com/", verified: true,
+        id: { apa: "Situs penjelasan xAPI: apa itu statement, apa itu Learning Record Store, dan apa bedanya dengan standar lama seperti SCORM.",
+          inti: ["Statement berbentuk pelaku-kata kerja-objek (actor-verb-object).", "LRS adalah tempat statement disimpan, terpisah dari LMS.", "xAPI bisa merekam pengalaman di luar kursus daring.", "cmi5 adalah profil yang menjembatani xAPI dengan alur kerja LMS.", "Kosakata yang terbuka berarti tim lo sendiri yang harus menyepakatinya."],
+          kenapa: "Ini pintu masuk paling ringan ke standar yang menentukan data apa yang tersedia untuk fitur AI di kemudian hari.",
+          hubungan: "Situs ini dikelola Rustici Software, vendor yang juga menjual produk terkait — isinya edukatif dan diakui luas, tetapi bukan dokumen standar resmi. Untuk teks normatifnya, lihat spesifikasi ADL di bawah. URL sudah dibuka dan diverifikasi pada sesi ini." } },
+      { title: "xAPI Specification (repositori resmi)", source: "ADL Initiative", topic: "AI + LMS", difficulty: "Advanced", minutes: 45, url: "https://github.com/adlnet/xAPI-Spec", verified: false,
+        id: { apa: "Repositori spesifikasi xAPI yang dikelola ADL Initiative, terbagi menjadi bagian pengantar, struktur data, dan pemrosesan serta keamanan data.",
+          inti: ["Bagian 2 mendefinisikan struktur statement secara normatif.", "Bagian 3 membahas pengiriman, validasi, dan keamanan data.", "Statement berformat JSON, jadi keterampilan Day 10 langsung terpakai.", "Spesifikasi juga sudah dibakukan sebagai standar IEEE.", "Dokumen rujukan, bukan bahan belajar berurutan."],
+          kenapa: "Saat vendor mengklaim 'xAPI compliant', dokumen inilah yang menentukan apakah klaim itu berarti sesuatu.",
+          hubungan: "Status 'perlu verifikasi': URL ini muncul dari hasil pencarian tetapi belum dibuka langsung pada sesi ini. Baca setelah materi pengantar di atas, bukan sebelumnya." } },
+    ],
+    quiz: [
+      { t: "mc", c: "SCORM vs xAPI", q: "What is the essential difference between SCORM and xAPI?", opts: ["xAPI is faster to load in a browser", "SCORM reports a small fixed set of outcomes to the LMS, while xAPI records open-vocabulary statements about experiences", "SCORM is proprietary and xAPI is not", "xAPI replaces the need for an LMS"], a: 1, e: "The difference is what each can express. A fixed vocabulary answers 'did they finish'; an open one can describe what happened, at the cost of agreeing that vocabulary yourself." },
+      { t: "mc", c: "LMS data", q: "What is a Learning Record Store?", opts: ["A marketplace for course content", "The database inside every LMS", "A separate system that receives and stores xAPI statements", "A backup of learner grades"], a: 2, e: "The LRS is deliberately separate from the LMS, which is what lets it record experiences that never happened inside a course." },
+      { t: "tf", c: "Where AI belongs", q: "Because a model can grade open responses, it is reasonable to let it hold the official grade in the LMS.", a: false, e: "Assistance and authority are different roles. A grade is a consequential decision about a person and needs evidence, appeal and a human signing it off." },
+      { t: "sc", c: "LMS data", q: "A team wants an at-risk learner model but only has SCORM completion percentages. What is the correct first move?", opts: ["Choose a more capable model", "Instrument the events that would actually carry the signal, then revisit the model", "Lower the threshold at which learners are flagged", "Train on historical completion data anyway"], a: 1, e: "This is a data problem wearing a model problem's clothes. No model recovers a signal that was never recorded." },
+      { t: "mc", c: "Learner privacy", q: "You plan to send learner free-text answers to a hosted model for analysis. What does this mean?", opts: ["Nothing, since the answers are not names", "A third party now processes personal data, so minimisation, retention and consent become deliberate decisions", "It is only a concern if the provider is outside your country", "It is safe if the data is sent over HTTPS"], a: 1, e: "Free text about a person is personal data, and transport encryption says nothing about what happens after arrival. Decide retention rather than inheriting it." },
+      { t: "sa", c: "LMS data", q: "In your own words: why do many 'add AI to our LMS' projects fail at the data layer rather than the model layer?", a: { groups: [["rekam", "record", "catat", "collect"], ["completion", "selesai", "skor", "score"], ["kasar", "coarse", "detail", "rinci", "granular"], ["instrumen", "instrument"]], need: 2, model: "What the LMS already stores — completion and a final score — is too coarse for most AI ideas, so the missing piece is instrumentation rather than a better model." }, e: "The idea is that the useful signal was never recorded, so the limiting factor is what you track, not which model you pick." },
