+      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
+        "Security is not a product you buy; it is a set of properties you decide to protect. The classic three, the CIA triad, are confidentiality (who may read it), integrity (who may change it, and would you know), and availability (can the people who need it reach it).",
+        "Naming which of the three is at stake ends most vague security conversations in one sentence. A leaked learner list is a confidentiality failure; a silently altered grade is an integrity failure; a ransomware lockout is an availability failure. The controls differ." ] },
+      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
+        "Authentication proves who you are. Authorisation decides what you may do once proven. They fail differently, and the second fails more often: broken access control sits at the top of the OWASP Top 10 for web applications — above injection — because it is easy to check a password and then forget to check whether this particular user may open this particular record.",
+        "On credentials, the practical order of value is not what most training says. Reuse is the real danger: a password leaked from an unrelated site years ago is tried against your accounts automatically, which is credential stuffing. Length beats complexity for what you must remember. Multi-factor authentication defeats most credential attacks outright, and a password manager plus MFA on your email account is the highest-value hour you will spend on this subject.",
+        "Phishing remains the main entry point, and the old advice has expired. Generative models write fluent, correctly-spelled messages in any language, so grammar is no longer a signal — Day 15 explains exactly why. The durable defence is procedural: verify unusual requests through a second channel you chose, not through anything contained in the message.",
+        "Encryption comes in two kinds that protect against different things. In transit, TLS protects data as it crosses the network (Day 4). At rest, disk and database encryption protects data on stolen hardware. Neither does anything against an attacker who logs in legitimately, which is why access control keeps returning.",
+        "Patching and supply chain: most compromises use vulnerabilities that were already known and fixed. Meanwhile the dependencies your project pulls in are code you did not write and rarely read, and a compromised package is now an ordinary attack route rather than an exotic one.",
+        "Backups are the only reliable answer to ransomware. The convention is 3-2-1: three copies, on two kinds of media, one of them off-site and disconnected. A backup you have never restored from is a hypothesis, not a backup." ] },
+      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
+        "Audit yourself, honestly. Check whether your primary email account has MFA enabled — it is the account that can reset all the others, so it ranks first.",
