+  {
+    day: 27,
+    title: "Workflow Automation and n8n",
+    sub: "automation",
+    objectives: [
+      "Describe an automation in terms of trigger, nodes, data and credentials.",
+      "Decide whether a task is worth automating, using frequency, time saved and error cost.",
+      "Design a workflow that fails loudly and safely rather than silently.",
+    ],
+    segments: [
+      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
+        "Automation tools connect systems that were never designed to know about each other. The vocabulary is small and worth learning once: a workflow is a set of nodes; a trigger node starts it; data passes between nodes as JSON items; credentials hold the authentication each node needs.",
+        "n8n is the tool this curriculum uses because it is visual, self-hostable, and shows you the data at every step — which makes it a good place to learn the concepts even if you end up using something else." ] },
+      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
+        "Triggers come in three practical kinds. A schedule trigger runs on a clock and polls: simple, but it asks 'anything new?' repeatedly and finds out late. A webhook trigger is the reverse — the other system calls your URL the moment something happens, which is faster and cheaper but requires that system to support webhooks and requires your endpoint to be reachable and secured. An app event trigger is a webhook the integration set up for you.",
+        "Most of the real work in a workflow is not calling APIs; it is reshaping data between two systems that disagree about field names, date formats and what counts as empty. Items are JSON, so Day 10's skills apply directly, and being able to read the data panel between two nodes is the core debugging skill here.",
+        "Design for failure, because the third-party system will be down at some point. Four things belong in any workflow you rely on: retries with a delay, idempotency so that running twice does not create two enrolments, an error branch rather than a silent stop, and a notification to a human. An automation that fails silently is worse than no automation, because you have already stopped checking manually.",
+        "Credentials are stored secrets, so Day 22 applies unchanged: scope them narrowly, rotate them, and never paste them into a node parameter where they will end up in an execution log.",
+        "AI nodes fit inside this structure rather than replacing it — classification, extraction and drafting are probabilistic steps surrounded by deterministic ones. Put the human approval step before anything consequential leaves the building.",
+        "Finally the decision rule, which matters more than the tool: automate when frequency multiplied by time saved exceeds build time plus ongoing maintenance, and when the cost of it doing the wrong thing is bounded. A task performed twice a year does not justify a workflow that breaks whenever a vendor changes an API." ] },
+      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
+        "Take one manual process you actually perform — enrolling new learners, assembling a monthly report — and map it on paper as trigger, then steps, then outputs.",
+        "Mark every point where data changes shape, and every point that depends on a system you do not control. Those two markings are where the work and the risk live." ] },
+      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
+        "Take the map you just drew and add the failure design: which step retries, which stops the run, and who gets told.",
+        "Write what happens if the workflow runs twice on the same input. If the answer is 'two of everything', you have found the idempotency problem before it found you." ] },
+      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
+        "Which step did you quietly assume never fails? That is the one that will surprise you, and naming it now is most of the fix." ] },
